title: install_signal_handlers permanently hijacks SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP with no uninstall
updated_at: "2026-10-26T00:13:15Z"
---
Found while root-causing B184. Confirmed independently by the coordinator on 2026-08-23.

src/hark/lifecycle.py:150 install_signal_handlers() replaces SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP with its own handlers and provides NO way to undo it. It is gated by a module global `_handlers_installed` (declared at :20, set True at :167) whose only effect is to make every later call a silent no-op:

    global _handlers_installed
    if _handlers_installed:
        return

Consequences:
1. Any consumer that runs the ambient loop even once can never get its own signal handling back for the life of the process. `request_shutdown` becomes the ONLY SIGINT semantics available.
2. The installed `_stop_handler` records a shutdown request and returns - it does not raise. So after installation, `os.kill(os.getpid(), SIGINT)` no longer produces a KeyboardInterrupt anywhere in the process.
3. The no-op guard means a caller cannot even re-assert its own handlers by calling again; the call succeeds and does nothing.

Measured blast radius in the test suite (B184 evidence): a per-test leak detector caught the SIGINT handler flipping to install_signal_handlers.<locals>._stop_handler at tests/test_config_watch.py::test_ambient_loop_file_watch_reload and STAYING for the following ~5300 test teardowns. That is mechanism M1(b) of B184 - the interrupt tests were signalling a process whose SIGINT no longer raised.

Library users are the real concern, not just tests: hark is importable, and importing plus running an ambient loop should not permanently commandeer the host process signal disposition.

Fix direction: add an uninstall/restore path that saves the previous handlers (signal.getsignal for each) at install time and puts them back, ideally via a context manager so the ambient loop scopes it. Decide whether the module global should become a saved-state object rather than a bool. This is an API change, which is why the B184 agent correctly left it alone.

Related: B185 (SigintMaskGuard.__del__ swallowing a genuine KeyboardInterrupt) is the same family of concern - process-wide signal state owned with no clean handback.

Verified 2026-10-26: handlers leak across ambient returns. Signal lane serial order B194 then B185 then B195 then B184; B189 independent within lane. Decision: scoped handler ownership/restoration; remove signal restoration from finalizers and reconcile in ordinary owner frames; common stdlib/libc pidfd adapter with stable real-capability testing. Coordinate tts_isolation/capture/tunnel adapters with other owners.

Implementation/decision receipt 2026-10-26: Install returns ownership acquisition; uninstall restores only still-owned handlers and complete signal transaction, ambient scope covers early returns/errors and nested/external replacements. Independent review and parent review completed; isolated-worktree code merged to master. Final combined remote gate pending.

Final verification 2026-10-26: reviewed fix merged to master. Integrated x-tall CPython3.13.11 suite: 6525 passed, 104 skipped, 0 failed in151.61s. Independent review and parent review passed. Full receipt /tmp/hark-b135-full-final.log; per-task evidence and design above.